 colony on a control plate is the same thing.</td></tr><tr><td class="">Grow to saturation. If a well has not grown, go back to picking or give the shaker more time — do not read a well that did not grow.</td></tr><tr><td class="b">CHECKPOINT</td><td class="">send this in before carrying on</td></tr><tr><td class="h">What to send</td><td class="">a photo of each plate before you picked, under every lighting the sheet names, and the colony counts</td></tr><tr><td class="h">Email it to</td><td class="">jca-cortex@berkeley.edu</td></tr><tr><td class="h">Put this line in the message</td><td class="e">cortex::lactis3_s10_pick_culture</td></tr><tr><td class="">That line is how the lab&#x27;s system knows which experiment and which step your message belongs to. Copy it exactly.</td></tr><tr><td class="b">Your notes</td></tr><tr><td class="">Anything that happened that the plan did not say. This comes back with the file and becomes part of the record.</td></tr></table></div></section><section id="assay"><div class="sh"><h2>Session 11 · Assay</h2></div><p class="meta">58 rows · <span class="flag">over one printed page (14 rows too many)</span></p><div class="scroll"><table><tr><td class="b">Assay for Experiment Lactis3</td></tr><tr><td class="b">Your name</td><td class="e"></td><td class="n"></td><td class="b">Date</td></tr><tr><td class="">protocol: plate_reader_fluorescence</td></tr><tr><td class="">source: read the next day, once the block is saturated</td></tr><tr><td class="b">Source</td></tr><tr><td class="h">what</td><td class="h">Box</td><td class="h">Well</td><td class="h">note</td></tr><tr><td class="b">lactis_cultures</td><td class="n"></td><td class="n"></td><td class="">made by the culture step that produces lactis_cultures</td></tr><tr><td class="b">Samples</td></tr><tr><td class="h">construct</td><td class="h">samples</td></tr><tr><td class="">lactis3_1_assay</td><td class="">L3h</td></tr><tr><td class="b">Plate Reader: Fluorescence and OD600</td></tr><tr><td class="">Read 6 cultures in 2 technical replicates on the Tecan M Nano.</td></tr><tr><td class="">Plan</td></tr><tr><td class="">- 6 cultures × 2 technical replicates = 12 wells of 96.</td></tr><tr><td class="">Procedure</td></tr><tr><td class="">1. Check the cultures grew and are fluorescent before you do anything else. A well that did</td></tr><tr><td class="">not grow is not a weak promoter, it is a failed culture, and the two look identical once the</td></tr><tr><td class="">numbers are in a spreadsheet.</td></tr><tr><td class="">2. Turn on the Tecan M Nano.</td></tr><tr><td class="">3. Using the P200 multichannel, transfer 100 µL of each culture into a</td></tr><tr><td class="">black-walled 96-well plate. Black walls stop optical crosstalk between wells.</td></tr><tr><td class="">- Work in columns of 8, so each culture is drawn 2 times.</td></tr><tr><td class="">- Budget about half a box of tips.</td></tr><tr><td class="">4. Read fluorescence. Settings:</td></tr><tr><td class="">- Fluorescence Top · excitation 483 nm · emission 525 nm · both bandwidths 20 nm</td></tr><tr><td class="">- Gain 40 (manual) · 20 reads · high-sensitivity flash</td></tr><tr><td class="">- Integration 40 µs · lag 0 µs · Z-position 11019 µm · target 30 °C</td></tr><tr><td class="">- Plate definition COS96ft</td></tr><tr><td class="">5. Also read OD600 — absorbance, 20 reads. Fluorescence without OD is uninterpretable,</td></tr><tr><td class="">because a brighter well may simply hold more cells.</td></tr><tr><td class="">6. Save the data to the memory stick. The instrument computer is not on the internet.</td></tr><tr><td class="">7. Shut down the instrument and the computer. Remove your plate first.</td></tr><tr><td class="">8. Clean up: bleach and rinse the plate. Either go on to miniprep, or put the culture</td></tr><tr><td class="">block in the fridge.</td></tr><tr><td class="b">For this experiment</td></tr><tr><td class="b">reporter</td><td class="">amilGFP</td></tr><tr><td class="b